---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

---

<Layout
  title="Solar Panel Racking & Mounting in Koh Chang, Thailand"
  description="Expert solar racking and mounting installation for all roof types in Thailand. Concrete, metal, and tile roof mounting systems engineered for tropical storms and monsoon winds."
>
  <!-- Breadcrumbs -->
  <nav class="breadcrumbs container" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <ol itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/BreadcrumbList">
      <li itemprop="itemListElement" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/ListItem">
        <a itemprop="item" href={`/`}><span itemprop="name">Home</span></a>
        <meta itemprop="position" content="1" />
      </li>
      <li itemprop="itemListElement" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/ListItem">
        <a itemprop="item" href={`/services/`}><span itemprop="name">Services</span></a>
        <meta itemprop="position" content="2" />
      </li>
      <li itemprop="itemListElement" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/ListItem">
        <span itemprop="name">Racking & Mounting</span>
        <meta itemprop="position" content="3" />
      </li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <!-- Hero Section -->
  <section class="hero hero--service">
    <div class="hero__content container">
      <h1>Solar Panel Racking & Mounting</h1>
      <p class="hero__subtitle">
        Engineered mounting systems for Thailand's roof types -- concrete, metal, and tile --
        designed to withstand tropical storms, monsoon winds, and decades of service.
      </p>
      <div class="hero__cta">
        <a href={`/contact/`} class="btn btn--primary">Get a Mounting Quote</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Stats -->
  <section class="stats container">
    <div class="stats__grid">
      <div class="stat-card">
        <span class="stat-card__number">180 km/h</span>
        <span class="stat-card__label">Wind Load Rating</span>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-card">
        <span class="stat-card__number">25+</span>
        <span class="stat-card__label">Year Structural Warranty</span>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-card">
        <span class="stat-card__number">100%</span>
        <span class="stat-card__label">Custom Fit for Thai Roofs</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Intro -->
  <section class="section container">
    <div class="section__content">
      <h2>Secure Your Solar Investment with Proper Mounting</h2>
      <p>
        The racking and mounting system is the structural foundation of every solar installation,
        yet it receives less attention than photovoltaic modules or inverters because it operates
        silently out of sight. A poorly designed or incorrectly installed mounting system can
        cause roof leaks, permit system failures under high winds, create shading that reduces
        panel output, or even allow arrays to detach during tropical storms. In Thailand,
        where monsoon winds regularly exceed 80 kilometers per hour and coastal properties
        face salt-laden air that accelerates corrosion, mounting quality is not negotiable.
      </p>
      <p>
        At Koh Chang Solar Cell, we design and install mounting systems specifically for
        Thailand's common roof types and environmental conditions. Our engineers assess roof
        structure, orientation, and wind exposure before specifying aluminum or galvanized
        steel rail systems, attachment hardware, and waterproofing methods matched to your
        specific building. We do not use universal mounting kits designed for temperate climates
        -- every installation uses components rated for tropical humidity, salt corrosion,
        and the wind loads specified in Thailand's building codes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Whether you have a reinforced concrete roof typical of modern Thai construction,
        a galvanized metal sheet roof common on commercial buildings and older homes, or
        traditional ceramic tile roofing that requires non-penetrating solutions, we have
        engineered mounting systems that secure your solar arrays without compromising roof
        integrity. Our installations include structural calculations signed by licensed Thai
        engineers where required by the Provincial Electricity Authority for net metering
        approval.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- How It Works -->
  <section class="section section--alt container">
    <div class="section__content">
      <h2>How Our Racking & Mounting Process Works</h2>
      <p>
        A proper mounting installation requires structural engineering knowledge, not just
        handyman skills. Our process ensures your solar arrays remain secure and your roof
        stays waterproof for the full 25-year panel warranty period and beyond.
      </p>
      <p>
        The process begins with a site survey and structural assessment. We inspect your
        roof's construction type, truss or beam spacing, and structural condition. For
        concrete roofs, we verify slab thickness and rebar placement to ensure chemical
        anchors achieve rated pull-out strength. Metal roofs require purlin spacing
        measurements to determine appropriate clamp placement. Tile roofs need assessment
        of batten condition and rafter spacing for hook-based mounting. We photograph all
        structural elements and note any pre-existing damage that should be repaired before
        solar installation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Based on this assessment, our engineer calculates wind loads using the 3-second
        gust speeds specified for your location under Thailand's building standards. Koh
        Chang's coastal exposure places it in a higher wind zone than inland areas, so our
        designs incorporate safety margins above code minimums. We specify rail profiles,
        clamp quantities, and attachment spacing that exceed calculated loads by a factor
        of 1.5 to 2.0, ensuring your system remains secure even in storm conditions that
        exceed typical expectations. The mounting structure is designed as a rigid, continuous
        frame that distributes wind loads across multiple attachment points rather than
        concentrating stress on individual fasteners.
      </p>
      <p>
        Installation follows a precise sequence. For penetrative mounts on concrete or
        tile roofs, we drill pilot holes, inject structural adhesive, and install stainless
        steel anchors with torque-controlled tightening to manufacturer specifications.
        Every roof penetration receives a multi-layer waterproofing system: butyl rubber
        gasket, aluminum flashing, and UV-resistant sealant rated for 25-year service life.
        Metal roof clamps use S-5! or equivalent clamp systems that grip the sheet profile
        without penetrating the roofing material, preserving waterproofing integrity.
        Rails are leveled to within 2 millimeters across the array to ensure even load
        distribution and proper drainage.
      </p>
      <p>
        Panel attachment uses grounding clips and end clamps with proper torque settings
        -- overtightening damages frames and undertightening allows movement during wind
        events. We maintain consistent gap spacing between panels for thermal expansion
        and airflow cooling, typically 10 to 15 millimeters depending on panel dimensions.
        Tilt angle is optimized for your latitude (approximately 12 degrees for Koh Chang)
        while respecting roof pitch and any shading considerations from parapets or adjacent
        structures.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Why Thailand -->
  <section class="section container">
    <div class="section__content">
      <h2>Why Quality Mounting Matters in Thailand</h2>
      <p>
        Thailand's climate and construction practices create mounting challenges that
        generic international products fail to address. Understanding these local factors
        explains why we invest in specialized hardware and installation techniques.
      </p>
      <p>
        Monsoon storms deliver sustained winds of 60 to 100 kilometers per hour with gusts
        exceeding 120 kilometers per hour during tropical depression passages. Standard
        mounting systems rated for European wind conditions fail at these loads. Our
        installations use rails and clamps tested to 180 kilometer per hour wind ratings
        with appropriate safety factors, and we never use plastic components for structural
        connections in tropical environments where UV degradation accelerates material
        failure.
      </p>
      <p>
        The combination of high humidity and coastal salt exposure causes rapid corrosion
        of unprotected steel hardware. We specify anodized aluminum rails (6005-T5 alloy)
        and stainless steel fasteners (A2-70 or A4-80 grade) for all installations, even
        inland properties where salt transport during storms creates corrosive conditions.
        Galvanized steel is used only where specifically appropriate, with HDG (hot-dip
        galvanized) ratings of G90 or higher rather than electro-galvanized alternatives
        that corrode within 5 years in Thai conditions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Thai roof construction differs significantly from Western standards. Concrete
        slabs in residential construction range from 100 to 150 millimeters thick, sometimes
        with unknown rebar placement that makes anchor depth unpredictable. Metal roofs use
        thin-gauge corrugated sheets mounted on widely spaced timber or steel purlins, creating
        limited structural attachment options. Ceramic tile roofs have steep pitches that
        create both wind uplift challenges and difficult working conditions. Our mounting
        solutions accommodate these realities with adjustable systems that work within
        structural constraints rather than forcing standardized Western hardware onto
        incompatible construction.
      </p>
      <p>
        Thermal cycling in Thailand -- where rooftop temperatures reach 70 degrees Celsius
        during hot season days and drop to 25 degrees Celsius during monsoon nights --
        creates expansion and contraction stress on mounting hardware. We design arrays
        with expansion joints on runs exceeding 10 meters and use sliding rail connections
        that accommodate movement without loosening. Rigid, fixed connections in long
        arrays develop stress fractures over time from this daily thermal cycling, a failure
        mode we eliminate through proper engineering.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What You Get -->
  <section class="section section--alt container">
    <div class="section__content">
      <h2>What You Get with Our Mounting Service</h2>
      <p>
        Our racking and mounting service delivers a complete structural solution with
        engineering documentation, quality materials, and installation workmanship that
        outlasts your solar panels themselves.
      </p>
      <p>
        We provide detailed structural drawings showing rail layout, attachment point
        locations, and hardware specifications. Where required for PEA net metering
        applications or building permits, these drawings carry a licensed Thai engineer's
        stamp. The documentation includes pull-test results for anchor installations,
        confirming that chemical or mechanical anchors achieve the manufacturer-specified
        withdrawal resistance in your specific concrete or masonry substrate.
      </p>
      <p>
        Materials supplied include all rails, clamps, brackets, anchors, flashings, and
        sealants necessary for a complete installation. We do not substitute generic hardware
        for specified components. Rail systems come from established manufacturers such as
        K2 Systems, Schletter, or equivalent tier-one suppliers with proven tropical
        installations. Stainless steel hardware is sourced from verified suppliers -- we
        have encountered counterfeit fasteners in Thai markets and verify material
        composition through spot testing. Standard residential mounting systems range from
        15,000 to 35,000 Thai Baht depending on array size and roof complexity, with
        commercial ground-mount systems quoted individually.
      </p>
      <p>
        Installation workmanship includes proper waterproofing at every roof penetration,
        torque-controlled tightening of all fasteners to manufacturer specifications,
        and post-installation inspection for rail alignment and panel spacing. We test
        grounding continuity across the array structure and verify that no sharp edges
        or protrusions remain that could damage panel backsheets or cable insulation.
        The completed installation is photographed from multiple angles for your records
        and warranty documentation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Structural warranty coverage extends 25 years on mounting hardware and 10 years
        on waterproofing integrity. If any attachment fails, any rail corrodes, or any
        roof leak develops at a mounting penetration within the warranty period, we repair
        it at no cost. This warranty is transferable to subsequent property owners, adding
        value if you sell your building. Annual inspection is included for the first three
        years to catch and address any developing issues before they become serious problems.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA Section -->
  <section class="cta-section">
    <div class="cta-section__content container">
      <h2>Ready for a Secure Solar Installation?</h2>
      <p>
        Don't risk your solar panels -- or your roof -- with substandard mounting. Get
        an engineered mounting system built for Thailand's climate and your specific building.
      </p>
      <a href={`/contact/`} class="btn btn--primary btn--large">Request a Free Quote</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
